<?xml version="1.0"?>
<mamelayout version="2">

    <!-- Rear white-ink print -->
    <element name="overlay_white_print">
        <image file="mine_back.png">
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1" height="1"/>
            <color red="1" green="1" blue="1" alpha="1"/>
        </image>
    </element>

    <!-- Tint of the transparent resin plate -->
    <element name="overlay_resin_color">
        <rect>
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1" height="1"/>

            <!--
                The resin's ONE-WAY transmission at unit optical density. #00D5FD = RGB(0, 213, 253).
                MAME takes colours in 0..1, so the figures below are the byte values over 255.

                Measured from IMG_3121.jpeg (2026-09-11). A linear 100/75/50/25/0% step wedge was
                displayed on an emissive panel with the real plate laid beside it and the pair
                photographed together. That is the same path the CRT light takes - once through the
                resin - so what comes out is the one-way transmission T itself, not the T^2 of a
                reflective measurement off white paper.

                The reading is a step-matching method that assumes nothing about the camera's
                response curve: find which step of the bare screen has the same code value as the
                plate-covered patch, and take the ratio of those steps as the transmission. Both the
                camera's tone curve and the display's gamma cancel, so the result does not depend on
                exposure or on HDR processing. The bare-screen value at the plate's own position was
                extrapolated by correcting the left-to-right viewing-angle gradient with a linear fit
                per band. Fitting transmission T plus an offset term gave R^2 = 0.98, so the model of
                a plain multiplicative filter holds up in the first place.

                  linear transmission   R < 0.02   G 0.674   B 0.983
                  sRGB code value       0.0000     0.8358    0.9922

                Red is effectively zero: darker than the bare black band (code values 10 to 17), so
                below the floor of the measurement. The real colour is a cyan outside the sRGB gamut
                - converting the original Display P3 reading to sRGB drives red negative - so red=0
                is the best approximation sRGB has.

                Green and blue came out close to the previous 0.8510 / 0.9216. It was only the
                previous red of 0.3530 that was wrong: the red component of the old round-trip
                reading #20b9d9, 32/255, was already near the measurement floor, and taking its
                square root amplified the error from 0.125 to 0.353. With red that far over, raising
                the density to compensate crushes blue instead - which is why no density ever
                matched.

                Alpha is the FRACTION OF THE PIXEL COVERED BY RESIN, and the plate covers all of it,
                so 1.0. At alpha=1 the mask's premultiplication does not quantise, the colour passes
                through exactly, and Overlay Color Optical Density becomes the effective density
                directly (a multiple of the plate's thickness, 1.0 = one measured plate). The colour
                here is the measurement itself, so density = 1.0 is one real plate.

                The shipped default is nonetheless 2.5. Held up against the real machine, green wants
                to be weaker than the measurement says. The measurement above used an LCD's green
                primary - a narrow band - as its source, while the real machine glows with the
                spectrum of CRT phosphor. A spectral filter's transmission does not carry across a
                change of source spectrum, so 0.674 measured against a TV's green cannot be taken
                over to the Vectrex's green unaltered. For this purpose the comparison against the
                real machine is the higher authority.

                With red at 0 and blue at 0.98, density is in practice a knob for green alone: from
                d=1 to 3.2 blue only moves 0.983 to 0.947 and red never leaves 0. At d=2.5 the
                effective transmission is R=0.000 G=0.373 B=0.958.
            -->
            <color red="0.0000"
                   green="0.8358"
                   blue="0.9922"
                   alpha="1.0"/>
        </rect>
    </element>

    <!--
        The plate's cut-outs: not something printed on it, but the shape it was cut away at.
        mine_holes.png was derived mechanically from mine_front.png - a flood fill picking up every
        pixel that is opaque (alpha>=250), near-black (RGB<=16) and connected to the image border.
        That comes to exactly five components (the top-centre half-disc at x693..922/y0..108 and the
        four rounded corners) and 23,344 pixels.

        Its RGB is white everywhere; only alpha describes the holes. That way, if the optical path is
        unavailable and the item is drawn as ordinary artwork, multiply resolves to dst*1 and the
        fallback is left intact.
    -->
    <element name="overlay_holes">
        <image file="mine_holes.png">
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1" height="1"/>
            <color red="1" green="1" blue="1" alpha="1"/>
        </image>
    </element>

    <!-- Surface colour print. The outer bezel is part of this one image. -->
    <element name="overlay_front_print">
        <image file="mine_front.png">
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1" height="1"/>
            <color red="1" green="1" blue="1" alpha="1"/>
        </image>
    </element>

    <view name="Vectrex Two-sided Overlay">

        <!--
            The whole view is laid out on the real overlay's 1613:2060. The screen aspect follows the
            driver and stays at 3:4 (400x300 shown ROT270).

            Its size comes from the WINDOW IN THE SURFACE PRINT, not from a percentage of the
            overlay's height. Sizing by height makes the screen narrower than the window and leaves a
            black band with no light in it inside the left and right bezel frames. Measured on
            mine_front.png, the window is x 54..1559 (1506 wide; the per-row minimum is 52).

              screen width  = 1512                      window width plus 2px of slack each side
              screen height = 1512 * 4/3 = 2016         keeps 3:4
              screen x      = (1613 - 1512) / 2 = 50.5
              screen y      = (2060 - 2016) / 2 = 22

            Top and bottom overshoot the window by about 29px and 123px, but the surface print is
            opaque there and hides it. With this rectangle there are no pixels that are inside the
            plate, transparent in the surface print and outside the screen - the previous
            1483.2x1977.6 left 42,440 of them, and they were the black band.
        -->
        <screen index="0">
            <bounds x="50.5" y="22" width="1512" height="2016"/>
        </screen>

        <!--
            Rear white-ink print. As below, blend is for the fallback only. White ink under multiply
            resolves to a factor of dst*(1+0) = 1.0 and disappears completely, so this one stays on
            alpha.
        -->
        <element ref="overlay_white_print"
                 optical-role="vectrex-white"
                 blend="alpha">
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1613" height="2060"/>
        </element>

        <!--
            Blue tint of the resin plate.
            While the BGFX Vectrex optical path is active this item is excluded from ordinary artwork
            drawing and its blend is never consulted (the optical-role skip in drawbgfx.cpp). Its RGB
            and alpha are read as an ink mask, and the alpha is multiplied by Overlay Color Optical
            Density (overlay_color_density) to give the optical density.
            The blend is for the case where the optical path is unavailable. A transparent resin plate
            is a passive filter and cannot add light, so multiply is the correct one; alpha would lift
            black areas toward blue.
        -->
        <element ref="overlay_resin_color"
                 optical-role="vectrex-color"
                 blend="multiply">
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1613" height="2060"/>
        </element>

        <!--
            Surface colour print. Ordinary artwork, visible with no light source behind it.
            The outer yellow frame, the PLAYER panels and the bottom bar are all part of this one
            image, so no separate bezel element is needed. A bezel-only image was tried and dropped:
            it was a proper subset of mine_front.png - zero opaque pixels the front print did not
            already have - and layering it only composited the antialiased edges twice and hardened
            them.
        -->
        <element ref="overlay_front_print"
                 optical-role="vectrex-front"
                 blend="alpha">
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1613" height="2060"/>
        </element>

        <!--
            The cut-outs, subtracted from all three masks: rear white ink, resin, and the shadow
            caster. Position in the file does not matter - the renderer punches them all at the end,
            in a pass of its own.

            Without this, the surface print draws the cut-outs as opaque black, so they enter the
            shadow caster as a plate that blocks room light. The shadow is displaced by the gap
            between plate and tube face (by default 2% of the face width times a rim scale of 1.7,
            about 51px), and the visible area begins 2px below the top half-disc, so a dark half-moon
            of roughly 230x51 lands inside the picture. The rear white print also has ink left inside
            the half-disc; it feeds the same caster and goes with it.

            The item itself is consumed by the optical path and never drawn normally. The cut-outs
            look black because the surface print draws them that way; this element takes no part in
            the appearance.
            Its blend is for the fallback only (the image is white throughout, so multiply is a no-op).
        -->
        <element ref="overlay_holes"
                 optical-role="vectrex-hole"
                 blend="multiply">
            <bounds x="0" y="0" width="1613" height="2060"/>
        </element>

    </view>

</mamelayout>
